From 4482266c40f6a89eb16e8da69d4d00500fb69e72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 21:09:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(hud): apply server element visibility restrictions

Apply the core's versioned HUD snapshot through direct connections and ViaProxy. Hide individual Java HUD draws without altering player settings or neighboring HUD elements. Preserve contextual progress backgrounds, fills, levels, and spectator hotbar behavior when visible.

Clear restrictions on world exit before another connection can render. Core owns packet decoding, cumulative restrictions, reset semantics, and late-registration snapshots. The client integration is necessary because Java has no standard packet for per-element HUD visibility.

Paper doll, touch-control, and control-hint restrictions remain retained by core. Java does not currently render those Bedrock HUD elements, so these hooks do not add substitutes or hide inventory tooltips.

Render the core locator-group snapshot with accepted Bedrock textures,
ARGB tint, actor binding, and distance-dependent position smoothing.
Keep invisible groups distinct from an empty stream, preserve default
Java waypoint behavior until a native snapshot arrives, and reject queued
payloads from a disconnected session.

Native reference: Bedrock 1.26.51.1, build 51061372, protocol 2193.
Controlled captures and native draw routine 0x148dc8e60 establish the
182-by-5 bar, strict 60-degree bearing range, size*5+2 icon extent,
FOV-dependent height arrows, fixed arrow geometry, overlap order, and
20-to-2 smoothing rates. Zero and negative size values remain valid.
Native resource initialization is at 0x148dcb400. Assets and captures stay
private; production resolves textures through accepted resource packs.

This implements the default native locator renderer. Full resource-pack
HUD definition overrides, legacy player-location presentation, and the
remaining native comparison matrix are still incomplete.
